We turn first to the Broken Power Law model (second panel in Fig. 3), which is characterized by two spectral indices, \\alpha_{1} and \\alpha_{2}, with p(m_{1})\\propto m_{1}^{-\\alpha_{1}} for m_{1}<m_{\\rm break}, and p(m_{1})\\propto m_{1}^{-\\alpha_{2}} above the break. We find the data prefer a break at m_{\\rm break}=39.7^{+20.3}_{-0.13}M_{\\odot}; 90% credible bounds on the location of this break are denoted by the gray vertical band in the second panel of Fig. 3. For masses above the break, the Broken Power Law model prefers a significantly steeper power-law slope, from \\alpha_{1}=1.58^{+0.82}_{-0.86} before to \\alpha_{2}=5.6^{+4.1}_{-2.5} after. Figure 5 shows the joint posterior on \\alpha_{1} and \\alpha_{2}. We infer that \\alpha_{2}>\\alpha_{1} at credibility 98%. The break aligns with the cutoff m_{\\rm max} inferred with GWTC-1 data (Abbott et al., 2019), and we speculate that the steep drop-off in the merger rate that occurs after m_{\\rm break} may be an imprint of PPSN, which are expected to become important for BH masses around 35\\,M_{\\odot}(Woosley and Heger, 2015; Heger and Woosley, 2002; Heger et al., 2003).

